Four items, in descending order of how much they matter.

---

## 1. The `floor` fill evicts models instead of deferring them — I deferred it (DESIGN FORK)

**§6's C6c `ceil` criterion is deliberately unsatisfied.** The plan says
`fairShareCap`'s `ceil` "must be **gone**, replaced by the whole-replica `floor`
fill." I implemented that first, measured it, and backed it out. The `ceil` is still
there, renamed `replicasToCover` and documented as a deliberate choice. **This is a
design fork, not a bug fix I folded in silently** — it is your call, and I am naming
it rather than shipping it either way without you.

### What I measured

I wrote the plan's `floor` (`wholeReplicaFill`) exactly as specified, then ran the
suite: **9 failures out of 334**. The deltas were:

| observed | expected | delta |
|---|---|---|
| `bv` | 6 | 2 | −4 |
| — | 3 | 2 | −1 |
| — | 4 | 3 | −1 |
| — | 4 | 3 | −1 |
| — | 3 | 1 | −2 |
| — | 2 | 1 | −1 |
| — | >1 | 1 | collapse |
| team-b | 2+ | 1 | collapse |

Several are **more than one replica**, and one (`bv` 6→2) is under an
*unconstrained* GPU budget. That is far outside the plan's stop condition ("one
replica at a mid-replica boundary"), so I did not touch a golden. I diagnosed
instead.

### Mechanism

`floor` is not a smaller allocation — it is a **termination**. In
`fairShareRolePick`, `capN == 0` returns `("", 0)`, so `allocated == false`, so
`fairShareScaleUp` sets `w.remaining = -1`, which drops the model out of `active`
**permanently**. It is never revisited, even as the mean falls in later rounds and
its entitlement grows past a whole replica. So a model behind the water level by
less than one replica's worth of GPUs is not deferred to the next round — it is
evicted from the cycle with zero replicas. That is the collapse-to-1 signature in
the table.

Under `ceil`, `target > 0` guaranteed `capN >= 1`, so every iteration made progress
and the loop always converged by allocating. `floor` removes that guarantee.

### Why I think the `floor` protects nothing

The `min` already has a floored term: `gpusAvail/gpusPR` is integer division on the
**real pool**, and it is the term that decides whether the hardware exists. Flooring
the *other* term — the entitlement — does not prevent overcommit, because the pool
term is what bounds physical GPUs. What flooring the entitlement decides is whether
a model owed a *fraction* of a replica may take the one indivisible unit that
allocation happens in. The entitlement is a fair-share water-level **gap**, not a
pool of GPUs on hand; rounding it up cannot commit a GPU that isn't there. And the
caller's own water-level check (`w.remaining > mean ⇒ -1`) is what stops the model
from taking a *second* replica.

So the two terms rounding in opposite directions is not the inconsistency the plan
worried the diff would look like — it is the rule the plan itself states, applied
per-term: **round up when asking how many replicas a demand needs, round down when
asking how many replicas a budget can afford.** The entitlement is the first
question; the pool is the second.

### What I need from you

Either (a) confirm the `ceil` stays and strike §6's C6c `ceil` bullet, or (b) keep
the `floor` and tell me what should happen to the evicted model — the honest fix is
that `capN == 0` must mean *defer*, not *evict*, which means `!allocated` can no
longer unconditionally set `remaining = -1`, and that is a change to the loop's
termination argument, not to the cap. It is more than a one-line commit and I would
want it as its own commit with its own convergence reasoning.

After restoring `ceil`, **exactly one** test was red: T1.4 — which §2d.6 already
schedules for rewrite in C6c. So the 9 failures decompose cleanly into 8 pure
rounding-boundary effects plus 1 piece of planned work. That is the proof the plan's
stop condition asks for.

